00:08:24.040 --> 00:08:26.660
Now here's something that most retail investors get wrong

00:08:26.660 --> 00:08:29.180
in it cost me money before I figured it out.

00:08:29.620 --> 00:08:32.660
Imagine you bought HPE shares yesterday morning

00:08:32.660 --> 00:08:33.960
after the earnings release

00:08:33.960 --> 00:08:36.960
when the stock was already up 20% pre-market.

00:08:38.000 --> 00:08:40.100
Your market order filled at the quoted price

00:08:40.100 --> 00:08:43.300
but the actual execution landed 3 cents worse

00:08:43.300 --> 00:08:45.800
than the last traded price shown on your screen.

00:08:46.520 --> 00:08:47.940
The mechanism is straightforward

00:08:47.940 --> 00:08:50.620
when retail flow hits a thinly traded name

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:51.920
during a catalyst spike.

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:55.600
Market makers widen the spread and absorb the imbalance

00:08:55.600 --> 00:08:58.240
so the effective fill price moves against you

00:08:58.240 --> 00:09:01.780
by the amount of that widening often 5 to 15 cents

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.220
on a fast moving stock.

00:09:04.000 --> 00:09:06.260
What most retail investors do not realize

00:09:06.260 --> 00:09:08.700
is that the displayed bid-ass quote

00:09:08.700 --> 00:09:10.320
is only the top of the book.

00:09:10.980 --> 00:09:12.880
The next levels can be much thinner

00:09:12.880 --> 00:09:15.340
and a single large order can walk the book

00:09:15.340 --> 00:09:16.860
several cents in seconds.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:20.020
The professionals always check level two depth

00:09:20.020 --> 00:09:23.300
and average daily volume before sizing a momentum trade

00:09:23.980 --> 00:09:26.340
then use limit orders or iceberg orders

00:09:26.340 --> 00:09:28.160
to avoid telegrafting size.

00:09:28.820 --> 00:09:30.720
The biggest mistake with order flow

00:09:30.720 --> 00:09:34.020
is assuming the quoted price is the price you will receive.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:36.640
Instead, always size positions

00:09:36.640 --> 00:09:39.920
so that even a 0.3% slippage hit

00:09:39.920 --> 00:09:42.600
still leaves the trade inside your risk budget.

